-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part sgh (wave 2; merged by the lead; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition -- THE SEAT LAYER of part sgh (ss 8 to 8H, 12 to 16A, 17 to 22).
--
-- SEAT: an individual who is, or is about to become, a director, the company secretary or a member (shareholder) of a company incorporated under the Act,
-- or of a foreign company registered under it, or who deals with one, asking on facts they can state what the Act requires or lets them do, what rights
-- they have against the company or its directors, what they must file, approve or vote on and by when, and what follows if it is not done.
-- (Assumed, not ruled: the Act is addressed to companies, so the individual is whoever stands in a company's management or ownership.)
-- There is never a seat for the Registrar, the Minister, the Authority or the Court: where a provision gives one of them a power or a duty, the question is
-- put as what that power or duty means for the individual ("may the Registrar ...", "must the Registrar ...", "may I appeal ...").
--
-- Every question is named "Under section N, ..." and asks what its body answers, in the same direction: JUST TRUE is yes, JUST FALSE is the text's own no,
-- NOTHING is "the text does not say". Where the layer-1 function is a bar, the question is put as the bar ("am I barred ...", "is the Registrar barred ...").
-- Nothing here is new law: each question calls one function of the layer-1 modules imported above and restates no rule.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Every input is a yes/no, a number, a string, a date, or a module's own choice (its constructors), one for each fact the person can state.
-- A date that the text allows to be absent (a longer period the Court or the Registrar allowed, a day on which a default was made good) is asked as two
-- inputs, "was there one" and "what is its date", and put back together inside the question.
-- The shared input names are the keel's: "the company is a private company", "the company is a public company", "the liability of the company's members"
-- and "the date asked" carry the same @desc wherever they are asked.
-- The terms of other parts (holding company, subsidiary, wholly-owned subsidiary, nominee, officer, private company) are asked as facts here, not worked out
-- from control links: the keel's seat answers them from a list of links (NOTES.md, Seams).

§ `Part 2: the Registrar's acts (section 8)`

-- calls layer-1: s 8(2) the act is as valid and effectual as if it had been done or signed by the Registrar
