-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part sgm (wave 2; merged by the lead; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition -- part sgm: THE SEAT LAYER (layer 2).
--
-- SEAT: an individual who is, or is about to become, a director, the company secretary or a member (shareholder) of a company incorporated
-- under the Act, or of a foreign company registered under it, or who deals with one. There is never a seat for the Registrar, the Minister,
-- the Authority or the Court: their powers and duties appear here only as what they mean to the individual ("must the Registrar update the
-- register", "may the Court order my company's name to be restored"). The questions are those the ranges of part sgm answer:
-- ss 189A to 198 (the registers of members and the annual return), ss 344 to 344H (striking off and restoring a company's name), and the
-- Fourteenth to Sixteenth Schedules (Part 11A: who has significant control).
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Every question takes plain inputs (yes/no, numbers, dates, and the modules' own choices), one for each fact the person can
-- state, each with an @desc worded as that person would be asked, and calls the layer-1 function of the same Act provision; no rule is
-- restated here. An input named for a fact has the same name, type and @desc in every question that asks it. Where a layer-1 function takes a
-- record (the entries of a register), the record is built inside the question from the yes/no facts.
--
-- READING A RESULT (the rule of 2026-10-29 and the rulings after it). A question that asks "is", "must", "may" or "does" answers JUST TRUE
-- for yes. JUST FALSE is the text's no, in its own words (a "means" definition, a condition introduced by "if" or "where", a period the text
-- fixes, a flat negative, "unless", "only"). NOTHING means the text does not say: it lists without closing the list, limits a grant or a
-- prohibition in its own wording and the case is outside the limit, or is silent. A name never asks the opposite of what its body answers:
-- a question asking whether a bar holds is titled as the bar ("is the Court barred from ...").
--
-- Dates are Date day month year. Money is dollars. The Registrar's, the Court's and the Authority's own judgments (whether the Registrar
-- thinks there are special reasons, whether the Court is satisfied) are plain facts the person states, not guessed.


§ `Part 5 Division 4: the register and index of members of a public company (sections 189A to 196)`

§§ `Section 189A: whether the Division applies`

-- section 189A(1): s 189A(1) this Division applies to the company
@export Under section 189A(1), does Division 4 of Part 5 apply to my company?
GIVEN `the company is a public company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the company a public company, as section 4(1) of the Companies Act uses the word?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 189A(1), does Division 4 of Part 5 apply to my company` IS
    `s 189A(1) this Division applies to the company` `the company is a public company`

§§ `Section 190: the register and index`

-- section 190(1): s 190(1) the company must keep a register of its members
@export Under section 190(1), must my company keep a register of its members?
GIVEN `the company is a public company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the company a public company, as section 4(1) of the Companies Act uses the word?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 190(1), must my company keep a register of its members` IS
    `s 190(1) the company must keep a register of its members` `the company is a public company`

-- section 190(1): s 190(1) the register is the register of members of the company kept pursuant to section 190
@export Under section 190(1), is this register the register of members of my company kept pursuant to section 190?
GIVEN `the company is a public company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the company a public company, as section 4(1) of the Companies Act uses the word?
      `the register is the register of members of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register is the register of members of your company?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 190(1), is this register the register of members of my company kept pursuant to section 190` IS
    `s 190(1) the register is the register of members of the company kept pursuant to section 190` `the company is a public company` `the register is the register of members of the company`

-- section 190(1): s 190(1) the register has every entry the subsection requires
@export Under section 190(1), does my company's register have every entry the subsection requires?
GIVEN `the register enters the names and addresses of the members` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register enters the names and addresses of the members?
      `the company has a share capital` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that your company has a share capital?
      `the register enters a statement of the shares held by each member, distinguishing each share by its number (if any) or by the number (if any) of the certificate evidencing the member's holding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register enters a statement of the shares held by each member, distinguishing each share by its number (if any) or by the number (if any) of the certificate evidencing the member's holding?
      `the register enters the amount paid or agreed to be considered as paid on the shares of each member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register enters the amount paid or agreed to be considered as paid on the shares of each member?
      `the register enters the date at which the name of each person was entered in the register as a member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register enters the date at which the name of each person was entered in the register as a member?
      `the register enters the date at which any person who ceased to be a member during the previous 7 years so ceased to be a member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register enters the date at which any person who ceased to be a member during the previous 7 years so ceased to be a member?
      `the register enters the date of every allotment of shares to members and the number of shares comprised in each allotment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the register enters the date of every allotment of shares to members and the number of shares comprised in each allotment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 190(1), does my company's register have every entry the subsection requires` IS
    `s 190(1) the register has every entry the subsection requires` `my register's entries`
    WHERE
        `my register's entries` MEANS `s 190(1) register entries` WITH
            `the register enters the names and addresses of the members` IS `the register enters the names and addresses of the members`
            `the company has a share capital` IS `the company has a share capital`
            `the register enters a statement of the shares held by each member, distinguishing each share by its number (if any) or by the number (if any) of the certificate evidencing the member's holding` IS `the register enters a statement of the shares held by each member, distinguishing each share by its number (if any) or by the number (if any) of the certificate evidencing the member's holding`
            `the register enters the amount paid or agreed to be considered as paid on the shares of each member` IS `the register enters the amount paid or agreed to be considered as paid on the shares of each member`
            `the register enters the date at which the name of each person was entered in the register as a member` IS `the register enters the date at which the name of each person was entered in the register as a member`
            `the register enters the date at which any person who ceased to be a member during the previous 7 years so ceased to be a member` IS `the register enters the date at which any person who ceased to be a member during the previous 7 years so ceased to be a member`
            `the register enters the date of every allotment of shares to members and the number of shares comprised in each allotment` IS `the register enters the date of every allotment of shares to members and the number of shares comprised in each allotment`

-- section 190(2): s 190(2) the company must alter the register to show stock instead of shares
@export Under section 190(2), must my company alter its register to show stock instead of shares?
GIVEN `the company is a public company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the company a public company, as section 4(1) of the Companies Act uses the word?
      `the public company has converted any of its shares into stock` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that your public company has converted any of its shares into stock?
      `the public company has given notice of the conversion to the Registrar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that your public company has given notice of the conversion to the Registrar?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
