-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part sgo (wave 2; merged by the lead; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, SSO informal consolidation in force from 6 May 2026 to 20 October 2026 (a rendering, not the
-- official text) -- THE SEAT LAYER for Part 12 and the First to Fifth Schedules (ss 386A to 411), part agent sgo.
--
-- SEAT: an individual who is, or is about to become, a director, the company secretary or a member (shareholder) of a company incorporated under the Act,
-- or of a foreign company registered under it, or who deals with one. (Assumed, not ruled: the Act is addressed to companies, so the individual is whoever
-- stands in a company's management or ownership.) There is never a seat for the Registrar, the Minister, the Authority or the Court: where a provision
-- gives the Court or the Registrar a power, the question is what that power means to the individual (may the Court relieve me, may I appeal).
--
-- Every question is named for its provision ("Under section N, ...") and asks what its body answers, in the same direction: JUST TRUE is yes, JUST FALSE is
-- the text's own no, and NOTHING is "the text does not say". Where the layer-1 function is a bar, the question is worded as the bar.
-- Every question takes plain inputs (yes or no, numbers, dates, and the modules' own choices), one for each fact the person can state, each with an @desc
-- worded as the person would be asked; the same fact keeps the same name and the same @desc in every question. The three layer-1 functions that take an
-- optional penalty or amount (s 407(2), s 408(1) twice) are asked as a yes or no plus a number, and the optional value is built inside the question.
-- A question never restates a rule: it calls the layer-1 function of the same provision.
--
-- Left out of this layer, each with the reason, so that nothing is silently dropped: definitions that only feed other rules (s 386A, s 387B(6), s 391(1A),
-- s 391(3), s 392(1), s 395(4), s 402(2), s 403(5), the First Schedule) are asked inside the questions that need them or not at all; and the regulative
-- rules (the duties and prohibitions, which are run as traces in the layer-1 tests) are not questions.

§ `Companies Act 1967 Part 12 and the Schedules: the questions of a director, company secretary or member`

§ `Part 12 Division 1: service of documents and electronic transmission (ss 387 to 387D)`

-- s 387 the document is served on the company  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387, is a document served on the company?
GIVEN `the document is left at the registered office of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the document left at the company's registered office?
      `the document is sent by registered post to the registered office of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the document sent by registered post to the company's registered office?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 387, is a document served on the company` IS
    `s 387 the document is served on the company` `the document is left at the registered office of the company` `the document is sent by registered post to the registered office of the company`

-- s 387A(1) the notice may be given, sent or served using electronic communications to the current address of the person  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(1), may a notice of a meeting be given to a person by electronic communications at the person's current address?
GIVEN `the notice of a meeting is required or permitted to be given, sent or served under this Act or under the constitution of the company by the company or the directors of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the notice of a meeting one that the Companies Act or the company's constitution requires or permits the company or its directors to give?
      `the person is a member of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person to whom the notice or document goes a member (shareholder) of the company?
      `the person is an officer or auditor of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person to whom the notice or document goes an officer or the auditor of the company?
      `the notice is given, sent or served using electronic communications to the current address of the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the notice given by electronic communications to the person's current address?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 387A(1), may a notice of a meeting be given to a person by electronic communications at the person's current address` IS
    `s 387A(1) the notice may be given, sent or served using electronic communications to the current address of the person` `the notice of a meeting is required or permitted to be given, sent or served under this Act or under the constitution of the company by the company or the directors of the company` `the person is a member of the company` `the person is an officer or auditor of the company` `the notice is given, sent or served using electronic communications to the current address of the person`

-- s 387A(2) the notice of a meeting is treated as given, sent or served by publication on a website  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(2), is a notice of a meeting treated as given to a person by publication on a website?
GIVEN `the company and the person have agreed in writing that notices of meetings required to be given to the person may instead be accessed by the person on a website` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the company and the person agreed in writing that notices of meetings may instead be read by the person on a website?
      `the meeting is a meeting to which that agreement applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the meeting one that the agreement covers?
      `the notice is published on the website such that it is or can be made legible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the notice published on the website so that it is, or can be made, legible?
      `the person is notified, in a manner for the time being agreed between the person and the company for the purpose, of the publication of the notice on that website` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the person been told, in the way agreed with the company, that the notice is published on the website?
      `the person is notified, in a manner for the time being agreed between the person and the company for the purpose, of the address of that website` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the person been told, in the way agreed with the company, the address of the website?
      `the person is notified, in a manner for the time being agreed between the person and the company for the purpose, of the place on that website where the notice may be accessed, and how it may be accessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the person been told, in the way agreed with the company, where on the website the notice can be found and how to open it?
      `the notice continues to be published on and remains accessible to the person from that website throughout the period beginning with the giving of that notification and ending with the conclusion of the meeting` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the notice stay on the website, and stay open to the person, from the day the person was told of it until the meeting ended?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 387A(2), is a notice of a meeting treated as given to a person by publication on a website` IS
    `s 387A(2) the notice of a meeting is treated as given, sent or served by publication on a website` `the company and the person have agreed in writing that notices of meetings required to be given to the person may instead be accessed by the person on a website` `the meeting is a meeting to which that agreement applies` `the notice is published on the website such that it is or can be made legible` `the person is notified, in a manner for the time being agreed between the person and the company for the purpose, of the publication of the notice on that website` `the person is notified, in a manner for the time being agreed between the person and the company for the purpose, of the address of that website` `the person is notified, in a manner for the time being agreed between the person and the company for the purpose, of the place on that website where the notice may be accessed, and how it may be accessed` `the notice continues to be published on and remains accessible to the person from that website throughout the period beginning with the giving of that notification and ending with the conclusion of the meeting`

-- s 387A(3) the day on which the notice is treated as given, sent or served  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(3), on what day is a notice published on a website treated as given to a person?
GIVEN `the notice of a meeting is treated in accordance with subsection (2) as given, sent or served on the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the notice treated, under the website rule, as given to the person?
      `the day of the notification mentioned in subsection (2)(d)` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the person told that the notice was published on the website?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `Under section 387A(3), on what day is a notice published on a website treated as given to a person` IS
    `s 387A(3) the day on which the notice is treated as given, sent or served` `the notice of a meeting is treated in accordance with subsection (2) as given, sent or served on the person` `the day of the notification mentioned in subsection (2)(d)`

-- s 387A(4) the notice specifies the matters or information that the subsection requires  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(4), does the notice that a notice of meeting is published on a website specify the matters or information that section 387A(4) requires?
GIVEN `the notice is given for the purposes of subsection (2)(d)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the notice the one that tells the person the notice of meeting has been published on the website?
      `the notice specifies the matters or information required for a notice of that type under any other provision of this Act or the constitution of the company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the notice give the matters or information that another provision of the Act, or the company's constitution, requires for a notice of that kind?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 387A(4), does the notice that a notice of meeting is published on a website specify the matters or information that section 387A(4) requires` IS
    `s 387A(4) the notice specifies the matters or information that the subsection requires` `the notice is given for the purposes of subsection (2)(d)` `the notice specifies the matters or information required for a notice of that type under any other provision of this Act or the constitution of the company`

-- s 387A(5) subsection (2) does not invalidate the proceedings of the meeting  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(5), does section 387A(2) not invalidate the proceedings of the meeting despite a failure to keep the notice on the website throughout the period?
GIVEN `the notice of a meeting that is required to be published and remain accessible as mentioned in subsection (2)(e) is published and remains accessible for a part, but not all, of the period mentioned in that paragraph` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the notice on the website, and open to the person, for part but not all of the period it had to be?
      `the failure to publish and make accessible that notice throughout that period is wholly attributable to circumstances which it would not be reasonable to have expected the company to prevent or avoid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the failure wholly down to circumstances that the company could not reasonably have been expected to prevent or avoid?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 387A(5), does section 387A(2) not invalidate the proceedings of the meeting despite a failure to keep the notice on the website throughout the period` IS
    `s 387A(5) subsection (2) does not invalidate the proceedings of the meeting` `the notice of a meeting that is required to be published and remain accessible as mentioned in subsection (2)(e) is published and remains accessible for a part, but not all, of the period mentioned in that paragraph` `the failure to publish and make accessible that notice throughout that period is wholly attributable to circumstances which it would not be reasonable to have expected the company to prevent or avoid`

-- s 387A(6) the company may take advantage of the subsection despite its constitution  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(6), may the company take advantage of section 387A despite its constitution?
GIVEN `the subsection the company wishes to take advantage of is subsection (1), (2), (3), (4) or (5) of section 387A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the rule the company wants to rely on one of subsections (1) to (5) of section 387A?
      `the constitution of the company contains a provision to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the company's constitution contain a provision to the contrary?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 387A(6), may the company take advantage of section 387A despite its constitution` IS
    `s 387A(6) the company may take advantage of the subsection despite its constitution` `the subsection the company wishes to take advantage of is subsection (1), (2), (3), (4) or (5) of section 387A` `the constitution of the company contains a provision to the contrary`

-- s 387A(7) the number or address is the current address of the person of the company  [sgo-service.l4]
@export Under section 387A(7), is the number or address the person gave the person's current address for sections 387A and 387B?
GIVEN `the question arises for the purposes of section 387A or section 387B` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the question about electronic notices or documents under section 387A or section 387B?
      `the number or address used for electronic communication has been notified by the person in writing to the company as one at which that notice or document may be sent to the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the person told the company in writing that the notice or document may be sent to this number or address?
      `the company has no reason to believe that that notice or document sent to the person at that address will not reach the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the company have no reason to think a notice or document sent to that address will fail to reach the person?
